00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
all right uh sandra we've put a panel  together general jack keen is with us  along with dan hoffman also joining us  this hour is brett bruin he served 12  years as a u.s diplomat as a former  obama white house director of global  engagement and brett why don't we start  with you since you're you're new to the  program this afternoon we heard what  putin had to say a lot of it was  lamenting the fall of the soviet union  that they gave away too much that  ukraine is a false entity that was  created by the former communists there  are a lot of complaints about ukraine  and its treatment toward russia ever  since uh yanukovych left in 2014 under  pressure i mean putin even going so far  is to say that russia will do everything  it can to punish those involved in the  fire in odessa in 2014 and that's when  pro-ukrainian protesters attacked a  building  in which there were pro-russian  ukrainians  burned the building and killed 30 people  inside i mean how do you punish people  responsible for a fire in odessa unless  you're planning on being in the entire  country  yeah it is really worrying what we heard  from vladimir putin this afternoon i  think in a lot of ways he's doubling  down on his threats and and clearly  trying to uh read off a laundry list of  grievances and complaints what i think  is the key question looming over  washington right now is how is president  biden going to respond and up until now  what the administration has said is  we'll slap on some more economic  sanctions but quite frankly after what  we heard from vladimir putin this  afternoon that is not enough he has  clearly not been deterred by what we've  already done and there are some decently  strong economic sanctions in place  already after his invasion of crimea and  luhansk and donetsk in 2014 so the  administration is going to have to put  more on the table they're going to have  to stiffen the spines of our european  allies because this simply cannot stand  john  uh very interesting dan we caught up  with you as we are beginning the show  one o'clock eastern time you've been  able to address a lot of what we've  learned since then especially from the  words of vladimir putin your thoughts  so what i'm waiting for now is the  response from ukraine they're the one  whose country is being dismembered you  know it started back in 2014 when when  putin annexed crimea and launched the  war in donbass and now vladimir putin is  saying that the don boss should be an  autonomous republic of russia and they  should have their independence  recognized that the question for me is  how does ukraine respond there have been  great divisions between the blind  administration and president zielinski  over the fact that we didn't implement  preemptive sanctions that might have uh  taken a step towards inducing russia to  withdraw some of those troops or perhaps  not to take the step that vladimir putin  took today the question for me is  whether ukraine plans to fight for this  territory and i'm sure that's something  that our team on the ground in la viv  unfortunately not in kiev uh is going to  be looking at with the government of  ukraine uh frankly this is not a time  for our embassy to to have departed uh  keefe and and and moved to la vie they  should be they should have remained in  kiev to do their diplomatic work it's  going to be far more difficult for us  right now to manage our affairs with  ukraine uh being so far to the west and  really out of the loop with all of the  government officials with whom we need  to engage at this very perilous moment  uh general keane let's just bring you in  here and put a couple of run a couple of  things by you  uh putin's saying if ukraine gets  weapons of mass destruction the global  situation will change drastically we  cannot ignore this he says ukraine is  flooded with western weapons in recent  months also saying nuclear weapons  amount to preparation for attack on  russia  uh he wants to keep ukraine from  becoming a threat to russia vis-a-vis  nato  read the tea leaves for us general where  do you think this is headed  well i think it's clear i mean  i read through uh  you know some of the analysis and the  snippets that came out of the speech on  reuters and  and it's clearly he's building a case  not to  just occupy the donbass region or even  build a land bridge uh to the east to  crimea  he's building a case to occupy ukraine i  mean that that's what that whole speech  was about  uh certainly not just a limited military  attack uh to  secure much of the territory already  owns and expanded  um and and i think that  was clearly identified by the united  states intelligence services and i agree  with the biden administration in  releasing that information to take away  putin's element of surprise and also to  take away any sense of legitimacy here  by us the united states conducting her  own information campaign  against him and i think we correctly  identified that his  strategic attempt here  is to take control of ukraine and and i  think that speech is really all about  that i mean you it's dripping with  uh the emotion that he feels  uh personally about ukraine  and that is what sandra so correctly  identified in the july 12 000 word a  memo much of it uh penned by himself  according to uh sources in his own  language  and and and secondly while  ukraine has ideological uh overtones to  it it's a democracy and he doesn't want  that certainly and he doesn't want that  kind of impact on his own people  it also has geopolitical issues because  he wants ukraine to look to russia and  not to the west but really when it gets  down to what he is talking about here  is that ukraine never should have been  separated from the soviet union it is a  part of russia and i'm taking back the  land that is rightfully ours  that is what this is about i believe it  will be a phased operation  we'll we'll see if that unfolds it'll  start  in a very  minimal limited way but it will likely  expand over a series of days and i  and i agree with you john i mean we're  waiting to see  uh what the biden administration is  going to do now  um they have forecasted this correctly  and they deserve credit for that but now  it's up to them what are they going to  do about it we already know  for a fact that they've squandered many  of the opportunities that were afforded  to them because this began in march so  our viewers recall with the 70 90 000  occupation last march of 2021  when they encroached on ukraine's border  and we did nothing  and this has been going on all that time  and i thought it was a test then 90 days  into the biden administration they were  preoccupied with a domestic program and  also with afghanistan decision to pull  out of afghanistan made in april  after that fiasco and i think putin  looked at that as we've said so many  times as fundamental weakness on the  part of the united states and nato he  came back  and doubled those forces  in the fall leading up to where we are  right now at the end of february hey  sean hannity here hey click here to  subscribe to fox news youtube page and  catch our hottest interviews and most  compelling analysis you will not get it  anywhere else

